    Keep byte-order sample access on the OS 26 baseline
    
    PR #5 adopted the Swift 6.4 byte-order span operations
    (RawSpan.load(fromByteOffset:as:_:) and OutputRawSpan.append(_:as:_:)).
    Those entry points are gated to Apple OS 27, so the manifest floor had to
    be raised to 27.0 to make them compile. That put the package on a beta SDK
    with no stable CI runner, and it is the only reason the floor moved.
    
    Assemble the two sample bytes directly instead. The contract requires an
    explicit byte order (MEM-01), not a particular standard-library type to
    express it, so nothing in the memory contract is weakened. The helpers keep
    both properties the span types supplied implicitly: alignment independence,
    since MEM-03 permits an odd plane offset, and a bounds check before the
    allocation is touched (MEM-04), since a raw buffer subscript is unchecked
    in release builds.
    
    Return the manifest to swift-tools-version 6.2 so a 6.2 or 6.3 consumer can
    still resolve the package. This is a resolution floor, not a ceiling; the
    sources build unchanged under 6.4.
    
    Verified: build and test pass under Swift 6.2.4 and 6.4.0, debug and
    release, 44 tests in 2 suites each. The existing golden-byte tests cover
    both endiannesses, 12-in-16 and full 16-bit precision, packed and padded
    rows, and reads at a deliberately odd physical address; a mutation that
    swaps the read byte order fails them. Rename the suite, which no longer
    describes anything version specific.
    
    Not changed here: the seven shared contract documents still state 27.0 and
    have already diverged from the other three repositories. POL-03 requires
    them byte-identical, so that correction belongs in a coordinated revision.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

